% ============================================================================
% metric-prefixes — each SI prefix → its power of ten (adj-facts-stdlib, METROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% The metrology (units-of-measure) fact that unlocks the metric system: a prefix
% names a power of ten. "kilo" means 10^3 (a kilometre is 1000 metres); "milli"
% means 10^-3 (a millimetre is a thousandth of a metre). Recall is a binding
% query — you ask for a prefix and get back its exponent, WITH its citation:
%
%     ? si_prefix_power(kilo, $P)       % 3
%     ? si_prefix_power(milli, $P)      % -3
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `si_prefix_power(prefix, power_of_ten)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% exponent WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not an SI prefix (it never invents an exponent).
%
% Why the `power` value is a NUMBER (and can be NEGATIVE): because it is a plain
% integer exponent, a recalled prefix COMPOSES straight into arithmetic — to scale
% a base quantity you multiply by 10^power. `milli` recalls as -3, so a millimetre
% is metres × 10^-3; `kilo` recalls as 3, so a kilometre is metres × 10^3. That is
% the concrete metrology bridge from RECALL (name the prefix) to COMPUTE (apply
% its power). Negative literals like `-3` are valid ADJ numbers, so the sub-unit
% prefixes are first-class rows, not a special case.
%
% Why a `table` and NOT twelve prose sentences: this is exactly the shape reference
% data ships in — a published prefix table, one prefix per row with its 10^n
% factor. A table cell like `kilo | k | 10^3` was NEVER a prose sentence, so there
% is no sentence to cite; the citable artifact IS the NIST prefixes table at the
% `locator` below, and each prefix→power pair here is read straight off it. The
% whole table is defended by one provenance envelope rather than repeating
% source/locator/trust on every row.
%
% Provenance: the prefixes and their powers of ten are taken VERBATIM from the NIST
% Office of Weights and Measures "Metric (SI) Prefixes" table. The `source` span
% below is the sentence on that page that states, in prose, that deka, hecto, and
% kilo mean 10, 100, and 1000 (i.e. 10^1, 10^2, 10^3); the remaining powers are the
% 10^n factors in the same page's prefix table. The prefix names use the NIST
% spelling (`deka`, symbol `da`). `trust authoritative` — a primary standards
% reference, re-fetchable at the `locator`. Nothing here is asserted from memory:
% every prefix→power pair is a cell of the cited table. Only prefixes whose power
% is confirmable on that page are included. (See ../README.md; ADJ-TABLES.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table si_prefix_power {
    columns prefix, power_of_ten

    % larger quantities or whole units (positive powers of ten)
    row (tera, 12)
    row (giga, 9)
    row (mega, 6)
    row (kilo, 3)
    row (hecto, 2)
    row (deka, 1)

    % smaller quantities or sub units (negative powers of ten)
    row (deci, -1)
    row (centi, -2)
    row (milli, -3)
    row (micro, -6)
    row (nano, -9)
    row (pico, -12)

    source "combining with the name of the unit the prefixes deka, hecto, and kilo meaning, respectively, 10, 100, and 1000"
    locator "https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/metric-si-prefixes"
    trust authoritative
}
